DO  -  10.1016/S1569-1993(23)00470-8
N2  -  Objectives: Pseudomonas aeruginosa (PsA) is the most prevalent pulmonary pathogen in cystic fibrosis (CF) patients ≥35 years old, with 50% of CF patients infected [1]. Bacteriophage (phage) therapy offers a novel alternative or adjunctive option to antibiotics in chronic PsA infections. The objectives of this Ph1b/2a study are to assess the safety and tolerability of nebulized phage (BX004‐A) and its effect on sputum PsA burden and clinical outcomes in CF subjects with chronic PsA pulmonary infection. Methods: This randomized, double‐blind, placebo‐controlled, multicenter study is evaluating nebulized BX004‐A in 32 outpatient adult CF subjects with clinically stable lung disease (FEV1 ≥40%) and chronic PsA pulmonary infections who are on inhaled antibiotics (tobramycin, aztreonam, or colistin) with all Screening PsA morphotypes susceptible to ≥1 phage in BX004‐A. In Part 1 (single‐ascending and multiple dose portion; n = 8), subjects are randomized (3:1) to BX004‐A or placebo x7 days, plus their usual inhaled antibiotic (D1–7). In Part 2 (multiple dose portion; n = 24), subjects will be randomized (2:1) to twice daily BX004‐A or placebo x10 days, plus their usual inhaled antibiotic (D1‐28). An independent Data Monitoring Committee oversees safety in both parts. Results: Nine subjects were randomized in Part 1. At Screening, mean age was 31.7 years (range 20–53), with 6/9 (67%) male, 9/9 (100%) white (not Hispanic/Latino), 3/9 (33%) on CFTR modulators, with a median sputum PsA level of 5.0 × 107 colony‐forming units/g (range 1 × 105–2.2 × 108). At D1, mean FEV1 was 66% (range 45%‐83%) and mean body mass index was 23.2 kg/m2 (range 18.7–26.7). Conclusion: This ongoing clinical trial is assessing the safety, tolerability, and efficacy of BX004‐A in CF subjects with chronic PsA pulmonary infection. All subjects in Part 1 had high levels of Screening PsA with all morphotypes susceptible to the BX004‐A phage cocktail.
