DO  -  10.1016/S1569-1993(23)01059-7
N2  -  Background: Pseudomonas aeruginosa is the most prevalent pulmonary pathogen in people with CF aged 35 and older, with half of people with CF infected [1]. Even after treatment with the most‐effective CFTR modulator available (elexacaftor‐tezacaftor‐ivacaftor), most people remain infected with P. aeruginosa [2]. Bacteriophage therapy offers a novel alternative or adjunctive option to antibiotics in chronic P. aeruginosa infections. The primary objective of this Ph1b/2a study is the safety and tolerability of nebulized phage (BX004‐A); exploratory objectives include the effect on sputum P. aeruginosa burden and clinical outcomes in peoplewith CF with chronic P. aeruginosa pulmonary infection. Methods: This randomized, double‐blind, placebo‐controlled, multicenter study is evaluating nebulized BX004‐A in 32 outpatient adults with CF with clinically stable lung disease (ppFEV1 ≥40%) and chronic P. aeruginosa pulmonary infections taking inhaled antibiotics (tobramycin, aztreonam, or colistin) with all screening P. aeruginosa morphotypes susceptible to one or more phages in BX004‐A. In Part 1 (single‐ascending and multiple‐dose portion), subjects were andomized (3:1) to BX004‐A or placebo for 7 days, plus their usual inhaled antibiotic (D1–7). In Part 2 (multiple‐dose portion; n = 24), subjects are randomized (2:1) to twice‐daily BX004‐A or placebo for 10 days, plus their usual inhaled antibiotic (D1–28). Subjects are followed for safety until 6 months after last dose. An independent data monitoring committee oversees safety in both parts. Results: Nine subjects were randomized in Part 1 (n = 7 on BX004‐A, n = 2 on placebo). At screening, mean age was 31.7 (range 20–53), six subjects (67%) were male, all were non‐Hispanic, three (33%) were taking CFTR modulators, and the median sputum P. aeruginosa levelwas 5.0 × 107 CFUs/ g (range 1 × 105–2.2 × 108). At Day 1, mean ppFEV1 was 66% (range 45–83%) and mean BMI was 23.2 kg/m2 (range 18.7–26.7 kg/m2). Topline results from Part 1 after the Day 15 visit showed that the study drug was well tolerated, with no adverse events related to study drug and no premature discontinuations from the study drug or study. Mean P. aeruginosa reduction at Day 15 (from baseline) was −1.42 log10 CFUs/g for BX004‐A vs. −0.28 log10 CFUs/g for placebo plus standard‐of‐care inhaled antibiotics (Figure 1). There was no emerging resistance to BX004‐A in treated subjects. Phages were detected in the sputum of all BX004‐A subjects during treatment period, including in several subjects up to Day 15. Enrollment in Part 2 is ongoing. Conclusions: This ongoing clinical trial is assessing the safety and efficacy of BX004‐A in people with CF with chronic P. aeruginosa pulmonary infection. All subjects in Part 1 had high levels of screening P. aeruginosa, with all morphotypes susceptible to the BX004‐A phage cocktail. Part 1 topline results showed that the study drug was well tolerated and had promising microbiologic efficacy in BX004‐A‐treated subjects. Acknowledgements: This work was supported by CFF [equity investment] and Israel Innovation Authority Grants 74353 and 77602. [Figure presented]
