DO  -  10.1016/S1569-1993(24)00853-1
N2  -  Background: Pseudomonas aeruginosa persists in most people with cystic fibrosis (PwCF) despite improvements observed with the most‐effective CF transmembrane conductance (CFTR) modulator (elexacaftor/tezacaftor/ ivacaftor [ETI]) [1]. Bacteriophage (phage) therapy offers a novel alternative or adjunctive option to antibiotics in chronic P. aeruginosa infections. The primary objective of this phase 1b/2a study was to assess the safety and tolerability of a nebulized phage cocktail (BX004‐A); exploratory objectives were to assess the effect on sputum P. aeruginosa burden and clinical outcomes in PwCF with chronic P. aeruginosa pulmonary infection. Methods: This randomized, double‐blind, placebo‐controlled, multicenter study evaluated nebulized BX004‐A in 43 outpatient adults with CF with clinically stable lung disease (FEV1≥40%) and chronic P. aeruginosa pulmonary infection taking inhaled antibiotics (tobramycin, aztreonam, or colistin) with sputum P. aeruginosa of ≥105 CFU/g, and with all screening P. aeruginosa morphotypes susceptible to one or more phages in BX004‐A. In Part 1 (single‐ascending and multiple‐dose portion; n = 9), subjectswere randomized (3:1) to BX004‐A or placebo for 7 days plus their usual inhaled antibiotic. In Part 2 (multiple‐dose portion; n = 34), subjects were randomized (2:1) to twice‐daily BX004‐A or placebo for 10 days plus their usual inhaled antibiotic. Subjects were followed for safety until 6 months after last dose. An independent data monitoring committee oversaw safety in both parts. Results: Mean sputum P. aeruginosa reduction at day 15 was −1.42 log (BX004‐A, n = 7) vs. −0.28 log (placebo, n = 2) CFU/g in Part 1. In Part 2 (n = 34), 23 subjects were randomized to BX004‐A and 11 to placebo. In subjects with quantitative sputum P. aeruginosa CFU at baseline, three of 21 (14.3%) on BX004‐A had a P. aeruginosa–negative sputum culture at day day 10 (end of treatment), vs 0/10 (0%) on placebo. Subjects on continuous inhaled antibiotics (rather than cycling or alternating regimens) had a mean sputum P. aeruginosa CFU reduction at day 10 of −2.91 log (BX004‐A, n = 7) vs −0.10 log (placebo, n = 5) (Figure 1); subjects on continuous antibiotics and ETI had a reduction of −4.82 log (BX004‐A) vs −0.11 log (placebo) (p < 0.05), and subjects on continuous antibiotics and ETI with baseline FEV1less than 70% had a reduction of −3.84 log (BX004‐A) vs −1.03 log (placebo), with a relative FEV1improvement of 8.89% at day 17 between groups (change from baseline 5.66% in BX004‐A vs −3.23% in placebo). For this subgroup (on continuous antibiotics, ETI, with FEV1<70%), there was also improvement according to two patient reported outcome questionnaires: improvement of 10.65 in CFQ‐R (respiratory domain) between groups at day 17 (change from baseline 1.39% in BX004‐A vs −9.26% in placebo) and of −11.46 in CFRSD‐CRISS between groups at week 3 (change from baseline −7.07% in BX004‐A vs 4.39% in placebo). During treatment, there were no adverse events of special interest or serious adverse events. All subjects have completed 6‐month follow‐up. Conclusions: BX004‐A showed favorable safety and notable microbiologic and clinical efficacy in Part 2, with more‐pronounced microbiologic reduction than in Part 1. Acknowledgements: This work was supported by the CFF equity investment and Israel Innovation Authority grants 74353 and 77602. Reference: [1] Nichols DP, et al. Pharmacologic improvement of CFTR function rapidly decreases sputum pathogen density but lung infections generally persist. J Clin Invest 2023 Mar 28:e167957. doi: 10.1172/JCI167957
