DO  -  10.1016/S1569-1993(23)00976-1
N2  -  Background: In adults with CF, Pseudomonas aeruginosa is the most common sputum pathogen and is increasingly multidrug resistant (MDR). To address this problem, we identified a novel approach that leverages the antibacterial properties of lytic bacteriophages (phages) to design a trial using inhaled single‐phage therapy, informed by the Center for Phage Biology and Therapy at Yale's experiences with compassionate use cases. Lytic phages are bacteria‐specific viruses that self‐amplify while killing bacteria. This study investigated the effects of phage therapy on sputum P. aeruginosa CFUs/mL and phage therapy safety. Secondary analysis is ongoing to study the effect on lung function and reducing P. aeruginosa virulence through evolutionary trade‐offs such as the anti‐inflammatory effects of reduction in pyocyanin production, impaired P. aeruginosa attachment in lungs, impaired biofilm production, and increased antibiotic sensitivity. Methods: CYPHY was a phase ½, single‐site, prospective, randomized, parallel, placebo‐controlled, double‐blinded trial. The study drug was Yale Phage Therapy 01 (YPT‐01), a nebulized single‐phage therapy determined for each subject using an algorithm that selects the best‐performing phage from among three candidate phages depending on baseline susceptibility testing. Eight clinically stable adult subjects with CF who were chronically colonized with P. aeruginosa were randomized after screening for susceptibility to YPT‐01. All subjects had been on stable CFTR modulator therapy for at least 5 months before randomization. Subjects nebulized study drug once daily for 7 days. Sputum and spirometry were obtained at baseline and follow‐up intervals. Sputum was processed to measure P. aeruginosa CFUs/mL. Adverse events were continually recorded for up to 6 months. Results: At the point of unblinding, we determined that four subjects had received phage therapy, and four had received placebo. Our primary endpoint was change in sputum P. aeruginosa CFUs/mL from baseline to Day 14, for which we saw no difference between groups. There was no difference in safety outcomes between the two groups over the 6 months that participantswere followed; a more‐detailed analysis of adverse events and pulmonary exacerbations is ongoing. For our secondary outcomes, we saw no difference between groups in lung function at any point during the month after treatment. Analysis of P. aeruginosa evolutionary trade‐offs is ongoing. Conclusions: MDR P. aeruginosa is increasingly common in CF and exceedingly difficult to manage. Therefore, development of novel therapeutic approaches that reduce infection with minimal side‐effects are highly desirable to the CF community. In this first‐in‐human inhaled phage–therapy clinical trial, there was no difference in bacterial titers, but inhaled phage therapy was shown to be safe. Secondary‐effect analysis suggests that a single‐phage therapy of P. aeruginosa can reduce virulence, has anti‐inflammatory potential in the lung, can impair. Our trial was limited by small enrollment numbers because of early closure due to the COVID‐19 pandemic and potential problems with phage stability. We propose that a larger cohort is needed to demonstrate efficacy. Acknowledgements: This workwas supported by CFF [KOFF20A0], and the Blavatnik Program (Yale University).
