N2  -  RATIONALE: In adults with cystic fibrosis (CF), Pseudomonas aeruginosa is the most common sputum pathogen and is increasingly multidrug resistant (MDR). To address this problem, we leveraged the antibacterial properties of lytic bacteriophages (phages) to design a trial using inhaled single phage therapy, informed by our experiences with compassionate use cases. This study investigated phage therapy safety and effects on sputum P. aeruginosa (CFU/ml). Secondary analysis included effect on lung function. Analysis of changes in P. aeruginosa virulence; reduction in pyocyanin production, impaired P. aeruginosa attachment, impaired biofilm production, and increased antibiotic sensitivity, is ongoing. METHODS: CYPHY was a phase 1/2 single‐site, prospective, randomized, parallel, placebo‐controlled, double‐blinded trial. The study drug was Yale Phage Therapy 01 (YPT‐01), a nebulized single phage therapy determined for each subject by an algorithm that selects the best performing phage from three candidate phages depending on baseline susceptibility testing. Eight clinically stable adult subjects with CF, on CFTR modulator, and chronically colonized with P. aeruginosa, were randomized after screening for susceptibility to YPT‐01. Subjects nebulized YPT‐01 once daily for seven days. Sputum P. aeruginosa CFU/ml and spirometry were obtained at baseline and follow‐up intervals. Adverse events were recorded for up to six months. RESULTS: At the point of unblinding four subjects received phage therapy and four placebo. There was no difference between groups in sputum P. aeruginosa CFU/ml change from baseline to Day 14. There was no difference in safety outcomes between the two groups over the six months that patients were followed; a more detailed analysis of adverse events and pulmonary exacerbations is currently ongoing. For our secondary outcomes we saw no difference between groups in lung function during the month following treatment. Analysis of P. aeruginosa evolutionary trade‐offs is ongoing. CONCLUSION: MDR P. aeruginosa is increasingly common and exceedingly difficult to manage. Therefore, development of novel therapeutic approaches that reduce infection with minimal side‐effects, are highly desirable. In this first in human inhaled phage therapy clinical trial there was no difference in bacterial titers, but inhaled phage therapy was shown to be safe. Secondary effect analysis suggests that a single phage therapy can reduce P. aeruginosa virulence and has anti‐inflammatory potential in the lung by reducing pyocyanin production. Our trial was limited by small enrollment numbers due to an early closure influenced by the COVID‐19 pandemic and potential issues with phage stability. We propose that a larger cohort is needed to demonstrate efficacy.
