DO  -  10.1002/hep.32188
N2  -  Background: Pathogenic Klebsiella pneumoniae (KP) strains could be a therapeutic target in inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) and primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC). A KP strain (KP2H7) from IBD and PSC patients induced moderate‐severe colitis in IL‐10‐/‐ mice 1. This first‐in‐human study evaluated the safety and tolerability of orally administered BX002‐A cocktail of bacteriophages (phages) targeting KP2H7 and assessed phage viability after passage through the gastrointestinal tract (GIT). Methods: This randomized, single‐blind, placebocontrolled study assigned 18 healthy adults in a ratio of 14:4 to BX002‐A or placebo, twice daily x 3 days in fed state. All subjects received oral esomeprazole 40 mg daily from Day (D) ‐3 to D3 to increase gastric pH for phage survival during gastric passage. All stool was collected from D‐1 to D6. Subjects were monitored in a phase 1 unit from D‐1 to D6 and had a phone call for safety at 14 and 28 days after last dose. Vital signs, laboratory tests and adverse events (AEs) were assessed. Phage viability was assessed in stool by analysis of phage forming units (PFU). Results: BX002‐A was welltolerated with similar rates of treatment‐emergent AEs (TEAEs) between BX002‐A (42.9%) and placebo (50.0%). There were no treatment‐related TEAEs (TR‐TEAEs) on BX002‐A; one subject on placebo had a TR‐ TEAE. There were no serious AEs or TEAEs that led to discontinuation of study drug or study, or clinically concerning vital signs or lab values. Phage were detected in stool for all subjects. Once phage began to be detected, it continued to be detected in all stool samples through D6. All subjects with samples at D4, D5 and D6 had phages detected, even after dosing completed at D3. The overall total PFU reduction in stool from total PFU orally administered (in BX002‐A cocktail) was assessed: in 92.9% of subjects, there was ≤2 log reduction in PFU, with 50.0% subjects having ≤1 log reduction in PFU. The median overall total PFU, across all stool samples for the 14 subjects on BX002‐A was 1.671E+10 PFU, several log greater than the expected bacterial burden of KP in IBD and PSC patients as measured in stool. Conclusion: BX002‐A was safe and welltolerated in healthy adults. High levels of viable phages were detected in stool, with minimal loss after passage through the GIT. Oral phage therapy should be further explored to assess its ability to reduce pathogenic KP in the gut and serve as a potential therapeutic in IBD and PSC patients.
