DO  -  10.1007/s13410-025-01585-9
N2  -  Background and Aims Background: Diabetic foot ulcers (DFUs) are a major complication of type 2 diabetes, with approximately half progressing to diabetic foot infections (DFIs) that can result in amputation and reduced quality of life. The rise of multidrug‐resistant pathogens, notably Klebsiella spp. and Pseudomonas spp., has limited treatment options. This pilot study explores bacteriophage therapy as an alternative therapeutic option. Aim To evaluate the effect of bacteriophage therapy on wound healing in patients with infected diabetic foot ulcers (DFUs) compared with Standard of Care (SOC) treatment. To assess clinical improvement in diabetic foot infections, as measured by changes in wound/ulcer healing from baseline to the end of the follow‐up period, compared with SOC. Materials and methods This was a pilot, randomized, controlled study conducted at Tertiary care centre for Diabetes, South India, evaluating the efficacy of bacteriophage therapy for the treatment of infected DFUs. Of 686 DFU patients screened, 155 had Klebsiella pneumoniae, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, or mixed infections; 25 patients were enrolled based on eligibility criteria with consent. Baseline demographics and clinical parameters, wound images, wound measurements, and biomarkers were recorded. The percentage of wound healing was calculated as:{(Initial wound area‐Final wound area) ÷ Initial wound area} × 100. Bacteriophages specific to K. pneumoniae and P. aeruginosa were isolated, propagated, purified, formulated (∼10 PFU/mL in PBS) and tested at Amrita School of Biotechnology, Kollam. Enrolled patients were randomized into two groups: Group 1 (SOC; n = 14): Received conventional DFU care. Group 2 (Bacteriophage Therapy; n = 11): Received topical bacteriophage preparations on infected wound area. The study period was up to 45 days or until complete wound healing, whichever occurred first. Results The baseline clinical parameters were similar across the two groups, with no statistically significant difference.Participant attrition included three dropouts in Group 1 and two in Group 2.The mean proportion of wound healing was 81.5% in the bacteriophage group compared to 25.9% in the SOC group (p=0.031). Notably, no patients in the bacteriophage group required amputation, skin grafting, or secondary suturing, whereas in the SOC group, two patients underwent amputation and two required skin grafting with secondary suturing.No adverse effects reported by the patients and clinicians. Conclusion This study demonstrates the potential of phage therapy used in conjunction with antibiotics, as compared to SOC in the treatment of DFUs. The findings demonstrated excellent tolerability and promising efficacy, supporting further evaluation in multi‐centre trials with larger study populations to establish safety, efficacy and regulatory guidelines for clinical practice.
