T1  -  The effect of Bacteriophage on Diabetic foot Ulcers
JA  -  https://trialsearch.who.int/Trial2.aspx?TrialID=IRCT20250108064318N1
PY  -  2025
C3  -  ICTRP IRCT20250108064318N1
M3  -  Trial registry record
N2  -  INTERVENTION: Intervention 1: Intervention group: For patients in this group, a phage mixture prepared at the Pharmacy School, which can be used in the form of a spray, will be applied to the wound site once a day during the changing of the dressing. The amount of administration will be proportional to the size of the wound and will be such that the phage mixture reaches all parts of the wound. Before administering the bacteriophage and also during treatment, a microbial culture will be prepared from the wound secretions. Treatment will continue until there is evidence of wound healing and control of infection in the wound. Intervention 2: Control group: Patients in this group will be selected in accordance with the patients in the intervention group in terms of wound severity and type of microbe in culture. Patients in this group will receive only standard treatment for diabetic foot ulcers. Standard treatment includes antibiotic therapy and appropriate dressing prescription. Visit intervals will be determined according to wound severity. Treatment will continue until the wound heals. CONDITION: E10.5 Foot ulcer in insulin‐dependent diabetic patients. ; Type 1 diabetes mellitus with circulatory complications PRIMARY OUTCOME: Wound severity index. Timepoint: Wound severity is checked every two weeks after the start of treatment. Method of measurement: Wound severity will be determined based on the Wagner criteria. INCLUSION CRITERIA: Individuals with diabetic foot ulcer Ulcers should be in grades 3 or 4 according to the Wagner classification
