DO  -  10.1007/s00249-017-1222-x
N2  -  We have developed a novel artificial binding protein scaffold for use as molecular recognition research tools as well as for diagnostics, imaging and therapeutic applications. Molecular recognition reagents are key tools used to study protein expression, localisation and interactions. Antibodies are the most common reagents with many being highly valuable, although some are less well characterised or suffer from stability or batch variability issues. Recently alternative binding proteins have been developed to complement antibodies. There are a number of well‐known alternative binding proteins but here we report on Affimer proteins as research reagents. Based on two randomised variable regions we have generated both one and two variable region phage display libraries of >1010 complexity. We have selected high affinity Affimers against >400 target proteins, peptides and chemical compounds, with our normal selection process for monoclonal reagents being 2‐3 weeks. Affimers can be engineered via an optional C‐terminal cysteine to allow a range of modifications or molecular fusions. They have been used in ELISA, western blot analysis, pull‐down assays, histochemical and immunofluorescence, super‐resolution imaging, as well as to inhibit protein‐protein interactions and to block protein activity. Highly specific Affimers can discriminate between highly related targets and, as they can be expressed in mammalian cells, can inhibit in vivo function in a highly specific manner. The binding interactions of Affimer with some target proteins have been characterised by X‐ray crystallography. Affimers are also being used for the development of biosensors particularly for point‐ofcare antimicrobial resistance applications. Thus Affimer proteins, as with other alternative binding proteins, represent complementary affinity reagents to antibodies for a range of biochemical, biophysical and cell biology applications.
