DO  -  10.1111/jgh.70341
N2  -  Introduction: Antimicrobial resistance (AMR) has emerged as a major public health challenge and gut colonization with multidrug‐resistant organisms (MDRO) is a risk factor for infection with MDR organisms. Fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) can serve as a novel approach to deplete MDR colonization, with bacteriophages playing a key role. There is no sham‐controlled trial evaluating the role of FMT in MDRO decolonization. Aim: A randomized, double‐blind, sham‐controlled trial to evaluate the effectiveness and tolerability of a single session of FMT in achieving MDRO decolonization or AMR gene depletion and its impact on the gut virome and microbiome in patients with gastrointestinal (GI) diseases admitted in inpatient ward or intensive care unit. Methods: Patients found to have MDR organisms on rectal swab culture post‐discharge, once off all antibiotics, were randomized 1:1 to receive either FMT via colonoscopy or a sham intervention (sigmoidoscopy with saline injection). Stratified randomization was done into three groups: pancreatitis, chronic liver disease, and other GI diseases. Rectal swabs and stool specimens were obtained four weeks post‐intervention to assess the presence of MDR organisms, AMR genes, and analyze gut virome and microbiome profiles. The trial was registered with CTRI (2022/07/043847). Results: 71 patients (mean age 40 + 13 years, 71.8% male, 30 pancreatitis, 30 chronic liver disease, 11 other GI diseases) were randomized to FMT (n= 38) and sham arms (n= 33). At 4‐week post‐ intervention, MDR decolonization or AMR gene depletion occurred in 76.3% of FMT recipients compared to 57.6% in the sham group (p= 0.09). Although a significant reduction in AMR genes was observed in the FMT arm compared to sham group (3.03 ± 2.07 to 2.24 ± 1.48, p= 0.02 vs. 2.58 + 1.73 to 2.06 + 1.75, p= 0.17), no significant difference in MDR decolonization rates was noted between the groups. Subgroup analysis revealed patients with acute pancreatitis achieved significant reduction in MDRO decolonization or AMR gene depletion in FMT group compared to sham group (81.2% vs. 42.8%, p= 0.03]. FMT was well tolerated in all but one patient, who developed diarrhea. Virome analysis revealed increased richness and evenness in the FMT group, with an abundance of CrAss phages (e.g., Burzaovirus and Klebsiella phages) and a decrease in specific phage families (e.g., Schiekvirus and Taranisvirus), although no such changes were observed in the sham group. Microbiome analysis showed a reduction in pathogenic bacteria such as Klebsiella and Escherichia in the FMT group, alongside increased abundance of beneficial, short‐chain fatty acid‐producing bacteria (e.g., Blautia, Agathobacter, and Ruminococcus). Conclusion: A single session of FMT did not significantly improve MDR decolonization, yet a significant effect on AMR gene depletion was seen, especially in patients with acute pancreatitis. FMT favorably modulated the gut virome and microbiome composition, highlighting its potential role in patients with GI diseases with MDRO colonization.
