N2  -  Background and Aims: BACKGROUND AND OBJECTIVE Standard and effective anticoagulant therapy is crucial to prevent stroke relative to AF, so exploring efficient risk factors to foundate accurate prediction model is of social and economic significance, the model can help clinical doctors to revise individualized treatment plan during early phage of stroke Methods: PATIENTS AND METHODS From December 2011 to December 2016, 335 patients with CCE were hospitalized at the Department of Neurology, Second Affiliated Hospital of Suzhou University, Suzhou, China. All patients were newly diagnosed with cerebral infarction. The follow‐up period was 3 months. Randomly divided into training set (n=227) and validation set (n=98) according to 7:3. We integrated molecular data with available prognostic factors in patients undergoing antithrombotic therapy to evaluate their impact on Rankin score at 3 months. Variables were filtrated by stepwise logistic regression, optimal subset method and lasso regression, logistic. ROC curve, calibration curve and Decision curve analysis were verified the model in validation set. Results: B S.E. Wald Sig. Exp (B) Thrombolytic 5.758 0.056 Anticoagulant 0.228 0.481 0.225 0.635 1.256 Antiplatelet 0.978 0.465 4.419 0.036 2.660 drinking ‐1.223 0.546 5.009 0.025 0.294 UA ‐0.003 0.002 3.620 0.057 0.997 CHADS2 0.716 0.228 9.822 0.002 2.045 NIHSS 0.228 0.034 45.209 0.000 1.256 Constant ‐3.882 1.135 11.696 0.001 0.021 Area under roc curve Training Dataset 0.850(0.800‐0.900) Validation Dataset 0.778(0.684‐0.871) Conclusions: CONCLUSION The pre‐treatment NIHSS score, previous drinking history, serum uric acid level, Chads2 score, and initiation of anticoagulant therapy within 4.5 hours in our model had good predictive value for Rankin score at 3 months.
