N2  -  Staphylococcus aureus is the leading cause of bacterial death worldwide. Despite its frequency and severity, however, surprisingly little is known about the optimal management of S. aureus bacteremia. A recent survey of over 2000 clinicians from over 70 countries revealed wide practice variation and only a handful of high quality clinical trials in S. aureus bacteremia had been conducted until recently. As a result, significant controversy remains over optimal diagnostic and treatment. Real world evidence derived from large clinical datasets have shown that several quality indicators, such as echocardiography, consultation by infectious diseases specialists, and utilizing beta lactam antistaphylococcal antibiotics when possible, are associated with additive benefits in patient survival. In recent years, several well‐designed clinical trials have been published for S. aureus bacteremia. These trials promise to significantly improve our management of S. aureus bacteremia. The SABATO trial compared 14 days of IV antibiotics to a switch to oral antibiotics for patients at low risk for complications. Although 93% of screened patients were excluded, raising questions as to generalizability, patients randomized to oral antibiotic switch had similar outcomes as those who received a full 14 days of IV antibiotics. The POET trial, published in 2019, evaluated a switch to oral antibiotics for patients with well‐controlled left sided infective endocarditis. Although streptococci predominated as causative pathogens, a total of 87 patients with methicillin susceptible S. aureus were enrolled. While the modest sample size of the S. aureus subgroup limited definitive conclusions, outcomes between the two arms were similar. Two trials tested adjunctive agents for S. aureus bacteremia. DISRUPT was a Phase 3 randomized controlled trial of the bacteriophage derived lysin exebecase added to standard care for complicated S. aureus bacteremia while SAFO randomized patients with S. aureus bacteremia to receive IV Fosfomycin in addition to usual treatment. Both failed futility analyses. The ERADICATE trial, which compared anti‐MRSA cephalosporin ceftobiprole to daptomycin for complicated S. aureus bacteremia, was the first double‐blind Phase 3 registrational trial to be conducted. Ceftobiprole was non‐inferior to daptomycin. Safety was acceptable for both antibiotics. The VIRSTA scoring system was shown to be the best prediction rule for ruling out endocarditis, with patients having a score of > 3 meeting criteria to undergo Transesophageal echocardiography. Finally, recent diagnostics such as microbial cell free DNA offer the potential to eventually personalize treatment of S. aureus bacteremia.
