N2  -  Introduction: Fecal Microbiota Transplantation (FMT) delivered through capsules is an effective therapy against recurrent Clostrdioides difficile infections (rCDI). Studies suggests that the viral part (mainly represented by bacteriophages) of donor stool plays an important role in FMT treatment effect[1]. Through filtration steps the viral fraction of donor stool can be separated from the bacterial part to produce material that can be capsulized and delivered as fecal virome transplantation (FVT) through capsules[2]. The goal of this study was to compare the effect of FVT capsules against FMT capsules as treatment of rCDI in a randomized controlled pilot trial. Aims &amp; Methods: In the VISION study treatment with FVT capsules was compared to the standard treatment of FMT capsules in 40 patients with first or second recurrence of C. difficile infection. Participants were randomized 1:1 to receive either one dose of FMT or FVT capsule treatment preceded by approximately 10 days of Vancomycin 125 mg x 4 daily or Fidaxomycin 200 mg x 2 daily. The same four fecal donors were used to produce FMT and FVT material. The primary endpoint was clinical resolution defined as resolution of diarrhea with ≤ 3 daily bowel movements or persistent diarrhea with a negative C. difficile test result, eight weeks following treatment. Patients receiving antibiotics as treatment of other infections were treated with concurrent Vancomycin and were defined as treatment failures in the Intention‐to‐treat analysis but were excluded from the per‐protocol analysis. Results: Forty patients were randomized and received FMT or FVT capsule treatment with no significant difference between groups in baseline characteristics (Table 1). The primary outcome was achieved by 70 % (14/20) in the FVT group and 75% (15/20) in the FMT group with no significant difference between groups (p = 1). Three patients were omitted from the per‐protocol analysis. Here the primary outcome was achieved by 73.7 % (14/19) in the FVT group and 83.3 % (15/18) in the FMT group with no significant difference between groups (p = 0.69). Conclusion: FVT capsules were effective as treatment of rCDI and were non‐inferior to FMT capsules. Larger trials are needed to further explore the efficacy of FVT capsules as treatment of rCDI.
